      "read_incident": {
        "incident": {
          "events": [
            {
              "at": "2026-08-03T19:53:06Z",
              "author": "shah9661",
              "id": "5053670502",
              "seq": 0,
              "text": "## Environment\n\n- OS: Windows\n- Python: 3.11.3\n- nltk: 3.10.1\n- regex: 2026.7.19\n\n## Steps to reproduce\n\n```powershell\ncd C:\\development\\ml-pipeline\npython -m venv venv\nvenv\\Scripts\\Activate.ps1\npip install nltk\npython -c \"import nltk\"\n```\n\nNote: venv is created **inside** the project root — this is the standard `python -m venv venv` convention recommended everywhere (official Python docs, most tutorials, etc.).\n\n## Expected behavior\n\n`import nltk` should succeed cleanly, since `regex` is properly installed via pip into the venv's `site-packages`.\n\n## Actual behavior\n\nTraceback (most recent call last):\nFile \"<string>\", line 1, in <module>\nFile \"C:\\development\\ml-pipeline\\venv\\Lib\\site-packages\\nltk_init_.py\", line 142, in <module>\nfrom nltk.text import *\nFile \"C:\\development\\ml-pipeline\\venv\\Lib\\site-packages\\nltk\\text.py\", line 24, in <module>\nimport regex\nFile \"C:\\development\\ml-pipeline\\venv\\Lib\\site-packages\\nltk\\inisec.py\", line 128, in find_spec\nraise ImportError(\nImportError: Blocked import of regex from current working directory for security reasons. Use '-P' or set PYTHONSAFEPATH to prevent Python from searching the current working directory.\n\n\nAlso tried `python -P src\\data_preprocessing.py` — same error, since this appears to be nltk's own meta-path finder (`inisec.py`), not CPython's own `-P`/`PYTHONSAFEPATH` mechanism.\n\n## Evidence this is a false positive\n\n```powershell\n> pip show regex\nLocation: C:\\development\\ml-pipeline\\venv\\Lib\\site-packages\n```\n\nNo stray `regex.py` exists directly in the CWD root — confirmed via `dir -Recurse regex*`, which only shows the package correctly inside `venv\\Lib\\site-packages\\regex`.\n\n## Root cause hypothesis\n\nThe venv (`C:\\development\\ml-pipeline\\venv\\...`) is nested inside the project's CWD (`C:\\development\\ml-pipeline`). The import guard in `nltk/inisec.py` appears to do a naive path-containment check (module resolves to *some path under CWD* → treat as \"imported from CWD\"), which incorrectly flags legitimate venv `site-packages` installs as suspicious, since they technically sit under the CWD tree.\n\nThis breaks the single most common venv layout in the Python ecosystem.\n\n## Additional note on documentation scope\n\n`SECURITY.md` / the `nltk.pathsec` documentation only describes protections for **NLTK's own resource loading** (`nltk.data.load()`, corpus/model downloads, zip extraction, pickle loading) — it says nothing about gating plain `import <package>` statements for third-party pip packages. This behavior appears to go beyond the documented scope of `pathsec`, either as an undocumented feature or a bug where import-time guarding and resource-loading guarding have become conflated.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\n- Exclude `site-packages` (and more generally, installed-package) directories from the CWD-containment check used by the import guard, since venv-inside-project is standard practice.\n- Alternatively, scope the guard to only flag files placed **directly** in the CWD root (loose `.py` files shadowing stdlib/third-party names), not nested venv installs.\n\nHappy to provide more logs/repro details if needed.",
              "url": "https://github.com/nltk/nltk/issues/3733"
            },
